WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:02.035 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'm a fan of yours
for a long time,

00:00:02.035 --> 00:00:03.303 line:92%
and I really enjoyed this film.

00:00:03.303 --> 00:00:04.371 line:92%
It's a strange, beautiful film.

00:00:04.371 --> 00:00:07.073 align:start size:94% position:6%
You speak of a creature
in the clip.

00:00:07.073 --> 00:00:09.175 line:92%
But tell us a little bit.

00:00:09.175 --> 00:00:10.610 align:start size:94% position:6%
Cold war.
-Yes.

00:00:10.610 --> 00:00:13.646 line:92%
It's set in the early '60s.

00:00:13.646 --> 00:00:17.517 align:start size:94% position:6%
The facility, the lab,
is in Baltimore.

00:00:17.517 --> 00:00:18.351 line:92%
Just outside of Baltimore.

00:00:18.351 --> 00:00:22.255 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, yes, my character,
I'm a colonel in the army.

00:00:22.255 --> 00:00:25.458
And I go down and I find this
thing in the Amazon River.

00:00:25.458 --> 00:00:28.928 align:start size:94% position:6%
And we're trying to figure out
what makes it work.

00:00:28.928 --> 00:00:30.463 line:92%
It's an amphibious being.

00:00:30.463 --> 00:00:32.832 align:start size:94% position:6%
It can be in the water
and the land.

00:00:32.832 --> 00:00:35.869 size:78% position:39%
And it's kind of tied
into the Cold War.

00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:39.105 align:start size:94% position:6%
The Americans and the Russians
trying to figure out

00:00:39.105 --> 00:00:39.806 size:84% position:42% line:92%
if there is anything --

00:00:39.806 --> 00:00:42.475 align:start size:94% position:6%
any kind of advantage they can
get from this creature.

00:00:42.475 --> 00:00:44.778 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You know, a lot of creatures
these days are CGI.

00:00:44.778 --> 00:00:46.246 align:start size:94% position:6%
That is not the case
in this film.

00:00:46.246 --> 00:00:47.647 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you were very cruel
to the creature,

00:00:47.647 --> 00:00:50.050 align:start size:94% position:6%
which may be difficult,
because this was an actual --

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:51.651 align:start size:94% position:6%
there was an actor
in here the whole time.

00:00:51.651 --> 00:00:54.954 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Oh, yeah, there was a fella
in there, Doug Jones.

00:00:54.954 --> 00:00:58.124 align:start size:94% position:6%
If you're familiar with
Guillermo del Toro's movies,

00:00:58.124 --> 00:01:01.961 size:88% position:44%
Doug has played several
creatures for Guillermo.

00:01:01.961 --> 00:01:04.197 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, "Pan's Labyrinth"
and "Hellboy."

00:01:04.197 --> 00:01:05.465 align:start size:94% position:6%
-The one with the eyes
in the hand.

00:01:05.465 --> 00:01:08.368 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't know if this means
anything to you.

00:01:08.368 --> 00:01:10.904 line:92%
But, yeah, Doug is kind of --

00:01:10.904 --> 00:01:13.373 align:start size:94% position:6%
I've described him
as Guillermo's monk.

00:01:13.373 --> 00:01:16.242 align:start size:94% position:6%
And Guillermo seems to like
that description.

00:01:16.242 --> 00:01:17.077 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
He kind of --

00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:21.648
Guillermo comes up with the most
fantastical beings imaginable.

00:01:21.648 --> 00:01:23.283 line:92%
And Doug makes them a reality

00:01:23.283 --> 00:01:25.718 align:start size:94% position:6%
along with, obviously,
the design of the costume.

00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:30.957
But I think the main pain Doug
experienced was in his knees

00:01:30.957 --> 00:01:35.695
because he had to spend a lot of
time on his knees in the lab.

00:01:35.695 --> 00:01:38.164 align:start size:94% position:6%
I don't think --
as far as I can tell,

00:01:38.164 --> 00:01:41.134 align:start size:94% position:6%
we don't have any sour grapes
between us.

00:01:41.134 --> 00:01:42.769 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Oh, that's good.
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:42.769 --> 00:01:43.636 line:92%
-Like, when it was all over,

00:01:43.636 --> 00:01:45.572 align:start size:94% position:6%
he didn't sort of
passive aggressively

00:01:45.572 --> 00:01:46.272 size:78% position:39% line:92%
talk about his knees?

00:01:46.272 --> 00:01:47.474 align:start size:94% position:6%
-No, no, no.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:47.474 --> 00:01:49.542 line:92%
He's a very thin, thin man.

00:01:49.542 --> 00:01:52.812 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the suit is, like,
made out of rubber.

00:01:52.812 --> 00:01:56.916
So it's basically like
kneeling on a dish sponge

00:01:56.916 --> 00:01:58.952 line:92%
for, you know, five hours.

00:01:58.952 --> 00:02:00.587 align:start size:94% position:6%
That's what I mean --
he's like a monk.

00:02:00.587 --> 00:02:03.123
He's just very, you know --
he can go to another realm.

00:02:03.123 --> 00:02:07.427
So me, like, prodding him is
not really that big a deal.

00:02:07.427 --> 00:02:11.998
-There are some beautiful
old Cadillacs in this film.

00:02:11.998 --> 00:02:14.767 line:92%
Your character purchases one.

00:02:14.767 --> 00:02:17.570 align:start size:94% position:6%
Is it true
that you actually, like,

00:02:17.570 --> 00:02:19.139 line:92%
ruined one of the Cadillacs?

00:02:19.139 --> 00:02:21.207 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, we had an incident
one night.

00:02:21.207 --> 00:02:24.344 align:start size:94% position:6%
There's a scene --
it's part of a chase sequence,

00:02:24.344 --> 00:02:27.647 align:start size:94% position:6%
where I'm trying to figure out
what's going on.

00:02:27.647 --> 00:02:29.649 line:92%
The creature has disappeared.

00:02:29.649 --> 00:02:31.751 align:start size:94% position:6%
And so my character
is in a mad dash

00:02:31.751 --> 00:02:33.186 align:start size:94% position:6%
going here, there,
and everywhere

00:02:33.186 --> 00:02:33.953 line:92%
trying to find the creature.

00:02:33.953 --> 00:02:37.690 align:start size:94% position:6%
And there's a scene
where I pull up in an old car,

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:42.428
and run into this building, run
up this fire escape in the rain.

00:02:42.428 --> 00:02:45.498 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's raining. It's night.
You know, it's very dramatic.

00:02:45.498 --> 00:02:48.668 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's an old car,
so the dash doesn't light up.

00:02:48.668 --> 00:02:52.405 align:start size:94% position:6%
So it's really hard to see
what gear you're in.

00:02:52.405 --> 00:02:53.072 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:02:53.072 --> 00:02:56.242 line:92%
So the second take we did,

00:02:56.242 --> 00:02:59.512 size:88% position:44%
I thought I clicked over
into park, but I hadn't.

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:00.380 size:78% position:39% line:92%
And I was halfway out

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:02.448 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I realized
the car was still rolling.

00:03:02.448 --> 00:03:04.984 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I tried to get my hand
back on the gearshift.

00:03:04.984 --> 00:03:07.053 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I was, like,
sliding onto the ground.

00:03:07.053 --> 00:03:10.557 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the car just very slowly
ran into this --

00:03:10.557 --> 00:03:13.526
like, a light post or something.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:13.526 --> 00:03:16.963 align:start size:94% position:6%
And ironically, they wound up
using the first take.

00:03:16.963 --> 00:03:17.497 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:17.497 --> 00:03:19.599
So didn't really even need to do
the second take.

00:03:19.599 --> 00:03:22.535 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Oh, gotcha. Yeah.
-It was totally unnecessary.

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:23.503 line:92%
But Guillermo did say that

00:03:23.503 --> 00:03:25.972
because I was able to get rid of
that light post,

00:03:25.972 --> 00:03:28.675 align:start size:94% position:6%
he was able to do another shot
he really wanted to do,

00:03:28.675 --> 00:03:31.511
that the light post had actually
been in his way.

00:03:31.511 --> 00:03:32.312 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Oh, wow.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:32.312 --> 00:03:35.648
So you actually did a great
favor to the production.

00:03:35.648 --> 00:03:36.216 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yeah, yeah.

00:03:36.216 --> 00:03:39.018
So if he wins, you know, best
director, he has me to thank.

00:03:39.018 --> 00:03:40.253 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:03:40.253 --> 00:03:41.888 line:92%
I've also read that you --

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:45.158
in between takes on film, which
can sometimes be really long --

00:03:45.158 --> 00:03:47.093
I don't think people quite
understand how much time

00:03:47.093 --> 00:03:50.163 align:start size:94% position:6%
is between the moments
you actually act.

00:03:50.163 --> 00:03:52.232 align:start size:94% position:6%
People think
you're in character,

00:03:52.232 --> 00:03:53.132 size:81% position:41% line:92%
but that is incorrect.

00:03:53.132 --> 00:03:54.968 align:start size:94% position:6%
-No, I'm just tired.
-Yeah.

00:03:54.968 --> 00:03:55.501 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:03:55.501 --> 00:03:56.936 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'm tired.
It's a really long day.

00:03:56.936 --> 00:04:02.175 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I find
small talk exhausting, so...

00:04:02.175 --> 00:04:05.311 align:start size:94% position:6%
Just kidding.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:05.311 --> 00:04:09.983 align:start size:94% position:6%
No, so I -- I try to just kind
of -- and also, you know,

00:04:09.983 --> 00:04:12.752
the thing about it is, like,
I'm used to doing theater.

00:04:12.752 --> 00:04:15.388 size:88% position:44%
I did a lot of theater
starting out in Chicago.

00:04:15.388 --> 00:04:16.089 line:92%
And I -- some people say,

00:04:16.089 --> 00:04:19.058 align:start size:94% position:6%
"How do you do something over
and over and over again?"

00:04:19.058 --> 00:04:19.959 size:81% position:41% line:92%
But I love repetition.

00:04:19.959 --> 00:04:23.029
Because as far as I'm concerned,
you never get it perfect.

00:04:23.029 --> 00:04:24.097 line:92%
You never get it exactly right.

00:04:24.097 --> 00:04:25.798 align:start size:94% position:6%
And there's something you're
always --

00:04:25.798 --> 00:04:26.532 size:84% position:42% line:92%
it's not so much, like,

00:04:26.532 --> 00:04:28.368 align:start size:94% position:6%
you can go deeper
in your character or whatever.

00:04:28.368 --> 00:04:30.937 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's just more like,
"What am I not noticing?

00:04:30.937 --> 00:04:32.672 align:start size:94% position:6%
What am I
not paying attention to?

00:04:32.672 --> 00:04:35.275 align:start size:94% position:6%
What other response
could I have?"

00:04:35.275 --> 00:04:38.211 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's just --
it's more like chess to me,

00:04:38.211 --> 00:04:41.714 align:start size:94% position:6%
trying to figure out the exact
way through it. Yeah.

00:04:41.714 --> 00:04:43.850 line:92%
-But do you find in film that --

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:47.320
Do you have the amount of
repetition you have in theater?

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:48.955 align:start size:94% position:6%
Or is it just --
-Sometimes.

00:04:48.955 --> 00:04:49.756 line:92%
Guillermo says, you know --

00:04:49.756 --> 00:04:53.159 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because Guillermo is a real
perfectionist, very obsessive.

00:04:53.159 --> 00:04:57.497
And he's like, "I may have met
my match in Michael Shannon."

00:04:57.497 --> 00:04:58.564 size:84% position:42% line:92%
He would be happy to --

00:04:58.564 --> 00:05:00.700 align:start size:94% position:6%
He's like, "We got it.
Let's move on."

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:01.501 size:78% position:39% line:92%
I'm like, "One more?"

00:05:01.501 --> 00:05:03.503 align:start size:94% position:6%
He's like,
"What are you going to do?"

00:05:03.503 --> 00:05:06.005 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I'm like, "I don't know!
Let's do it again!"

00:05:06.005 --> 00:05:09.342
-Meanwhile, Doug's on his knees
being like, "One more?"

00:05:09.342 --> 00:05:11.611 align:start size:94% position:6%
-He's like, "Yeah, 10 more!
Please!

00:05:11.611 --> 00:05:14.380 line:92%
I don't need these things."

